-- Making chosen system calls fail on purpose, for checks. Requires
-- lib/linux.herb; no main.
--
--   let f = fault_new()
--   do fault_fail(f, 74, 5)
--   do fault_start(fault_ready(f))
--
-- From then on every fsync (74) this program makes gives error 5 (EIO), and
-- is not done. Linux does this with a seccomp filter: a short list of
-- instructions Linux runs at every system call, which answers "give this
-- error instead" or "go on". Only the calls named in a rule are touched.
--
-- What it assumes: Linux on x86-64 with seccomp filters built in, as stock
-- Ubuntu and GitHub's runners have (Linux 4.14 or later); no administrator
-- rights, since no_new_privs is set first; and no filter already on this
-- program that refuses seccomp itself (a container's might). Once on, a
-- filter stays for the rest of the program and for every process it starts
-- and program those run, through fork and execve. Linux's own rules
-- (seccomp(2)): it cannot be taken off, and a later fault_start adds another.
-- Each call of a named number is answered the same way every time: there is
-- no "only the second time" (put the filter on at the right moment, or test
-- an argument, instead), no slower or shorter answer, and no record of the
-- calls made. A rule on a call the program itself needs, such as write or
-- close, needs an argument test (fault_fail_equal, fault_fail_above) to leave
-- its other uses alone. Error 0 makes the call give 0 without being done. It
-- is for checks, not a fence: it holds nothing against a program that tries
-- to get round it.
--
-- Numbers from Linux's headers: linux/seccomp.h, linux/filter.h,
-- linux/bpf_common.h, linux/audit.h, linux/prctl.h, asm/unistd_64.h.
-- https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/seccomp.2.html

-- code holds the instructions, 8 bytes each (a 16-bit operation, two 8-bit
-- jumps forward, a 32-bit value), room for 516; used is how many are written.
-- program is the 16 bytes Linux is given: the count at 0, the address of
-- code at 8.
ref record Fault:
    code: buffer
    used: int
    program: buffer
end

-- One instruction at the end of f. The operations used: 32 loads a 32-bit
-- word of what Linux tells the filter (the call's number at 0, the kind of
-- call at 4, argument i at 16 + 8i, its low half first); 21 jumps on equal,
-- 37 on greater; 6 answers.
func fault_put(f, operation, yes, no, value):
    let at = f.used * 8
    do linux_put16(f.code, at, operation)
    do buffer_set(f.code, at + 2, yes)
    do buffer_set(f.code, at + 3, no)
    do linux_put32(f.code, at + 4, value)
    f.used = f.used + 1
    return 0
end

-- A new Fault that fails nothing yet. Its first three instructions stop a
-- program that calls Linux the 32-bit way, which numbers its calls
-- differently. Allocates.
func fault_new():
    let f = Fault(linux_buffer(4128), 0, linux_buffer(16))
    do fault_put(f, 32, 0, 0, 4)
    do fault_put(f, 21, 1, 0, 3221225534)
    do fault_put(f, 6, 0, 0, 2147483648)
    return f
end

-- Whether a rule of size instructions with error fits and can be answered.
func fault_room(f, error, size):
    return error <= 4095 and f.used + size < 516
end

-- Every call number makes gives error, and is not done. 0; or 0 - 22 when
-- error is past 4095 or f is full (64 rules always fit).
func fault_fail(f, number, error):
    if not fault_room(f, error, 3): return 0 - 22 end
    do fault_put(f, 32, 0, 0, 0)
    do fault_put(f, 21, 0, 1, number)
    do fault_put(f, 6, 0, 0, 327680 + error)
    return 0
end

-- The same for only the calls whose argument arg (0 to 5, in the order of
-- the call's man page) is exactly value, all 64 bits of it.
func fault_fail_equal(f, number, error, arg, value):
    if arg > 5 or not fault_room(f, error, 7): return 0 - 22 end
    do fault_put(f, 32, 0, 0, 0)
    do fault_put(f, 21, 0, 5, number)
    do fault_put(f, 32, 0, 0, 20 + arg * 8)
    do fault_put(f, 21, 0, 3, value >> 32)
    do fault_put(f, 32, 0, 0, 16 + arg * 8)
    do fault_put(f, 21, 0, 1, (value & 4294967295))
    do fault_put(f, 6, 0, 0, 327680 + error)
    return 0
end

-- The same for only the calls whose argument arg is above value, both read
-- as unsigned 64-bit numbers: a descriptor above 2 is any but the first three.
func fault_fail_above(f, number, error, arg, value):
    if arg > 5 or not fault_room(f, error, 8): return 0 - 22 end
    do fault_put(f, 32, 0, 0, 0)
    do fault_put(f, 21, 0, 6, number)
    do fault_put(f, 32, 0, 0, 20 + arg * 8)
    do fault_put(f, 37, 3, 0, value >> 32)
    do fault_put(f, 21, 0, 3, value >> 32)
    do fault_put(f, 32, 0, 0, 16 + arg * 8)
    do fault_put(f, 37, 0, 1, (value & 4294967295))
    do fault_put(f, 6, 0, 0, 327680 + error)
    return 0
end

-- The address of the finished filter, for fault_start: "go on" is put after
-- the rules, so a call no rule names is done as usual. Rules may be added
-- after, and fault_ready called again.
func fault_ready(f):
    do fault_put(f, 6, 0, 0, 2147418112)
    f.used = f.used - 1
    do linux_put64(f.program, 0, f.used + 1)
    do linux_put64(f.program, 8, buffer_address(f.code))
    return buffer_address(f.program)
end

-- Put the filter at program (fault_ready's answer) on this process for good:
-- first no_new_privs, which lets a user who is not the administrator set a
-- filter, then the filter. 0, or Linux's raw -errno, and then this filter is
-- not on. It allocates nothing and touches no storage, so a new process made
-- by fork can call it just before execve, to give the program it starts the
-- filter: check its answer there, and do not start the program when it is
-- not 0. The filter answers the execve too, and everything the new process
-- does until then: a rule on write, say, also fails a starter's own report
-- that the program could not be started.
func fault_start(program):
    let quiet = linux_syscall6(157, 38, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0)
    if linux_error(quiet): return quiet end
    return linux_syscall6(317, 1, 0, program, 0, 0, 0)
end
